Because there’s often so many factors that can drive business, it can be overwhelming to know where to start digging for answers. I find it helpful to generate a list of mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive (MECE)[ probable] hypotheses to easily identify the top contenders and eliminate those that are less likely. This planning and forethought always[ saves me] time because it provides a[ targeted] list of analyses to consider, and every analysis assists in[ either] proving or disproving the most likely[ hypothetical] causes. By setting myself[ up] for success on the front end,[ I] find more time to[ generate] insights and[ implications] on the back end.
It always helps to understand the whole picture. It’s not enough to say that a brand is growing 10%. I always make data points relevant to my audience by benchmarking it back to something. Is that 10% accelerated or[ de]celerated from the prior[ year]? Is that 10% higher or lower than the category? Is that 10%[ better] or[ worse] than[ projected estimates]?[ The easiest] way to[ make] data mean[ something is] by[ making a] comparative “than”[ statement].
Visuals are the KEY to bringing data to[ life] and telling the story. As data analysts, it’s our job to make the numbers meaningful. We’re doing the data a[ dis]service every time we copy and paste a raw data chart from Excel onto a slide. To[ mitigate] this, I find it most helpful to ask myself, which hypothesis will[ I] demonstrate here? (See Tip #1.) Then my follow-up question is, what kind of chart or visualization is going to best represent this data and make the point clear? The simplest rule to follow is to visualize one point per slide. Finally

